module lib/hf/mole
  A molecule the way PySCF takes one: the atoms as text and the basis set by
  its name, `molecule("O 0 0 0; H 0 -0.757 0.587; H 0 0.757 0.587", "6-31g")`.
  The text is read by lib/xyz, the name by lib/basis/named, and the
  `Molecule` is built by lib/hf/molecule's `molecule_from` (or
  `molecule_bohr`), so it is the same molecule the array form builds, bit
  for bit.

mol_coverage  @ff921535
  why   The error for an element a basis set lacks says which elements it
        does cover, so the reader can pick another set.
  b     the basis set, one row per shell, sorted by element
  s     the shell being looked at
  zs    the atomic numbers the set has shells for, each once, in order
  last  the index of the last of them
  names the element symbols, when the numbers have a gap
  z     one atomic number of zs

mol_is_bohr  @a98eb678
  unit  the unit as the caller wrote it: "angstrom", "bohr", "B", "AU", ...
  u     the unit without surrounding spaces, in capitals

molecule_in  @2a5e24f2
  how   Read the unit; refuse a text with no atoms; read the atoms; look the
        basis set up by name; refuse an element the set has no functions for;
        build the molecule from the atomic numbers and positions.
  why   The text form builds the array form's molecule, bit for bit
        [test "water from text is water from atomic numbers, in angstrom and in bohr"].
  atoms  the atoms as text, one `symbol x y z` per atom
  basis  the basis set's name, as PySCF writes it
  unit   the unit of the coordinates, angstrom or bohr
  bohr   `1` when the coordinates are in bohr, `0` for angstrom
  g      the parsed atoms: symbols, atomic numbers `z` and coordinates `xyz`
  b      the basis set looked up by name
  i      the atom being checked against the basis set

molecule  @7a3488e0
  atoms  the atoms as text, in angstrom
  basis  the basis set's name, as PySCF writes it
